<p>Rapid central nervous system penetration, lethal chemical warfare&#x000a0;toxins.</p>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>G-series toxic agents are generally volatile, lethal inhalational toxins. Onset within seconds to minutes.</p></list-item><list-item><p>V-series agents are lethal, have low volatility, are highly skin-absorbable, and persist on surfaces.</p></list-item><list-item><p>A-series agents are highly lethal, resist oxime reactivation, and can evade protective gear.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 163px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Irreversible acetylcholine inhibitors</p>
<p>(pesticides)</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 187px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Agricultural pesticides</p>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Parathion</p></list-item><list-item><p>Methyl-parathion</p></list-item><list-item><p>Chlorpyrifos</p></list-item><list-item><p>Diazinon</p></list-item><list-item><p>Acephate</p></list-item></list>
<p>Volatile household pesticide</p>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>DDVP, also known as dichlorvos&#x000a0;(fumigation)</p></list-item></list>
<p>Moderate toxicity</p>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Malathion</p></list-item><list-item><p>Topical malathion (FDA- approved, but still can cause poisoning if ingested)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 310.028px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Classic organophosphate pesticide poisoning presents with miosis, salivation, lacrimation, urination, diaphoresis, gastrointestinal symptoms, and emesis.</p>
<p>Malathion's moderate toxicity is due to rapid liver carboxylesterase metabolism.</p>
<p>Topical malathion is FDA-approved for the treatment of pediculus humanus capitis (head lice and their ova) on the scalp.</p>
</td>
              </tr>
